<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dutch–Hanseatic War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1438">1438</a>–<a href="/years/1441">1441</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>–Hanseatic War was a naval and trade war from 1438 to 1441 between the Wendish towns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, and the towns of Holland and Zeeland, which were challenging Hanseatic control of the Baltic trade. Privateers on both sides attacked shipping. The Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> of 1441 opened the Baltic to Dutch merchants, marking the decline of Hanseatic dominance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-hanseatic-war.jpg" alt="Dutch–Hanseatic War" width="320" height="244" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flo Beck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haupthandelsroute_Hanse.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war was fought over trade and the control of Baltic shipping. For years tension had grown as Hanseatic merchants tried to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> small, and the Dutch had begun to use privateers against German ships. The County of Holland acted independently of the rest of the Burgundian Netherlands, of which it was then part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 14 April 1438 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> authorities declared war on the six Wendish cities, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneburg">Lüneburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anklam">Anklam</a>, and on the County of Holstein. On 23 April the League told its members and advised them to ship goods through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> rather than Holland or Zeeland. In May the Dutch mobilised every suitable ship in Holland and ordered 79 new vessels to be built.</p><p>There was little real fighting. For much of 1438 to 1440 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet patrolled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oresund">Øresund</a>, but the Wendish fleet blocked the passage and kept it from going far into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. The State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> declared itself neutral, and the towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> refused to give military help to the Hanseatic side. The opening for peace came in 1440, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a> was forced from the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. His successor, Christopher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, though nominally allied with the Hanseatic cities, wanted a settlement and gave Dutch merchants the same rights in Denmark as Hanseatic ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Peace was made in September 1441 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-copenhagen-1441">Treaty of Copenhagen</a>. Each side granted the other free trading rights, which gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> unrestricted access to Baltic trade once more and worked in the long run to their benefit rather than that of the German cities. The settlement also obliged the Dutch to pay costly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dutch–Hanseatic War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
